<div class="main">
<div class="page-header">
  <h1>Designing Product Interaction</h1>
  <p>Personal course notes. Add screenshots as you go — placeholders show where each one goes.</p>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s1">
  <span class="kicker">Section 1</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Introduction to Design Thinking</h2>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Design thinking for problem solving</h3>
    <p>Five stages from the Stanford d.school: empathise, define, ideate, prototype, test. Humans are at the centre throughout. Problem statements have four parts: the problem, the persona, the outcome, and how to measure it. No solutions in the problem statement — the process leads to conclusions without assumptions. Iterative — repeat as needed.</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Screenshot: five-stage design thinking diagram</p><code>mv ~/Desktop/design-thinking.png ~/Documents/Code/portfolio/src/assets/images/</code></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Case study: SaaS product</h3>
    <p>Course uses an HR consultancy building a SaaS tool as the running example. Starts with an ambiguous brief. Key point: ambiguous problems are normal in product development. Design thinking connects user behaviour to product experience.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s2">
  <span class="kicker">Section 2</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Product Definition, Ideation, and Frameworks</h2>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Design thinking for product development</h3>
    <p>Design fits in the first three phases: discovery, define, design. Works ahead of development — sometimes by a few sprints. Poor planning causes usability issues, poor metrics, and limited scalability. In agile: design requirements established, design created, tested with users, then handed off as epics broken into stories.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: design process in agile delivery diagram</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Understand customer behaviors and goals</h3>
    <p>Qualitative + quantitative data together give the full picture. Analytics shows what is happening. Interviews reveal why. Survey first to segment roles, then interviews, then contextual inquiry. Spend 20 minutes after each interview capturing key takeaways while fresh.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Identify and synthesize customer insights</h3>
    <p>Affinity mapping: one insight per sticky note, cluster into themes, let themes emerge from data rather than imposing them. If themes are not clear, go back and interview more people. Synthesis reveals research gaps as much as patterns.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: affinity mapping example from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Develop customer personas</h3>
    <p>Research-based illustrative models. Components: name, occupation, goals, tasks, pain points, opportunities. Provisional personas work without research — best guesses supplemented by data. Primary use: alignment across the team.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: persona template from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Visualize customer journey maps</h3>
    <p>Capture phases, activities, pain points, emotions, quotes, opportunities, metrics. Primary use: identifying gaps and pain points. If information is missing, go back and talk to users.</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Screenshot: journey map example from course</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s3">
  <span class="kicker">Section 3</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Uncovering and Identifying Business Problems</h2>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>User centered product requirements</h3>
    <p>Requirements derived from user research rather than business assumptions. Iterative feedback loop with real users. Traditional project management is business-first and linear. User-centred requirements ensure people will actually want to use the product — it is created with them in mind.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Ideation for user experience</h3>
    <p>Divergent process first — generate as many ideas as possible without constraints, time-boxed to 15-20 minutes. Low fidelity sketches to move fast. Group ideation with dot voting. Then converge — pick strongest ideas and add detail. Do not constrain yourself too early.</p>
    <div class="scroll-placeholder">
      <div class="item"><p>Sketch 1 — add ideation screenshot</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Sketch 2 — dashboard layout</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Sketch 3 — alternative concept</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Plan user flows</h3>
    <p>Map every decision point and connection before designing screens. Boxes and arrows. Site map tracks pages and views — user flow adds decisions and logic. Both are needed. Flows reveal gaps and logic problems before detailed design work begins.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: user flow diagram from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Gathering feedback</h3>
    <p>Stay low fidelity so feedback shapes the design before you invest in it. Sketches are enough to get meaningful reactions. Ask open-ended questions, avoid leading questions. You just need enough to get reactions. Fail fast: get wrong answers early when they are cheap to fix.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s4">
  <span class="kicker">Section 4</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Usability and Design Paradigms</h2>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Information architecture</h3>
    <p>How people find and move through a product — global navigation, page-level navigation, search, site maps. A good IA is invisible. Global navigation is accessible everywhere. Page navigation organises content within a defined area.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: site map or IA diagram from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Cognitive load and Gestalt principles</h3>
    <p>Cognitive load: mental effort required to process a page. Too many steps, too much content, poor layout all increase it. Gestalt principles are how the brain naturally organises visual information — design with them, not against them.</p>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Proximity</span><span class="desc">Elements close together are perceived as related. White space creates grouping without visible borders.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Similarity</span><span class="desc">Elements that look alike are perceived as functioning alike. Consistent colour, shape, size signal relationships.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Closure</span><span class="desc">The brain completes incomplete shapes. A line graph does not need a solid line to read as continuous.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Enclosure</span><span class="desc">A bounded area is perceived as a group. Cards and containers work because of this.</span></div>
    <div class="placeholder md" style="margin-top:1rem"><p>Screenshot: Gestalt principles diagrams from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Usability factors</h3>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Design patterns</span><span class="desc">Repeatable solutions to common problems. Reduce cognitive load through familiarity. Must suit specific context.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Information architecture</span><span class="desc">Poor IA makes things unfindable regardless of visual design quality.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Flow</span><span class="desc">If the sequence does not match expectations, usability suffers even with well-designed individual screens.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Visual design</span><span class="desc">Colour, typography, iconography, layout. Affects both desirability and usability.</span></div>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.88rem;color:#666">Measurement: heuristic evaluations, surveys, formal usability testing.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Fitts's law, affordance, and guiding principles</h3>
    <p>Fitts's Law: time to reach a target depends on size and distance. Interactive elements must be large enough to tap accurately — especially on mobile.</p>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Affordance</span><span class="desc">Appearance suggests function. Shadows and gradients on buttons signal they are pressable.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Consistency</span><span class="desc">Same patterns throughout. Users learn the system once. Core argument for design systems.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Mental models</span><span class="desc">Users bring expectations from other products. Match them for immediate intuitiveness.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Feedback</span><span class="desc">Every action gets a response — loading states, hover states, confirmations.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Error prevention</span><span class="desc">Design so errors are unlikely. When they occur, users can reverse them without damage.</span></div>
    <div class="placeholder sm" style="margin-top:1rem"><p>Screenshot: Fitts's law diagram from course</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="s5">
  <span class="kicker">Section 5</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">User Interface Design and Prototyping</h2>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Fundamentals of user interface design</h3>
    <p>Visual hierarchy established through position, size, weight, and colour. Important things go first. Without hierarchy, users cannot tell what to look at first.</p>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Typography</span><span class="desc">Size and weight create reading order. Same weight everywhere makes nothing stand out.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Colour</span><span class="desc">Draw focus or de-emphasise. Never colour alone — support with text and icons. Too much colour increases cognitive load.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">Grid</span><span class="desc">12-column grid standard for web. White space between columns separates elements.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><span class="name">White space</span><span class="desc">Active design element. Reinforces grouping and reduces cognitive load.</span></div>
    <div class="scroll-placeholder">
      <div class="item"><p>Position hierarchy example</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Size hierarchy example</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Typography hierarchy</p></div>
      <div class="item"><p>Colour hierarchy</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>UI components and elements</h3>
    <p>Buttons, forms, sliders, drawers — building blocks of interfaces. Design systems collect these with documentation: what they are, when to use, when not to, error states, anatomy, context. A Figma component maps to a coded component — shared source of truth.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: component documentation example from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Low fidelity design with Figma</h3>
    <p>Paper Wireframe Kit from Figma Community — intentionally rough so people focus on interactions, not visuals. Work loosely. Goal: get the flow and navigation structure right before layout decisions are made. Navigation first, content second.</p>
    <div class="placeholder md"><p>Screenshot: paper wireframe kit in Figma from course</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Interactive prototyping with Figma</h3>
    <p>Connect screens with triggers. Smart Animate detects matching layers and animates differences — reinforces feedback principle. Four screens with a few connections is enough to test with users.</p>
    <div class="placeholder sm"><p>Screenshot: Figma prototype mode showing screen connections</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="principles">
  <span class="kicker">Quick reference</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Psychology principles</h2>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Hick's Law</span><span class="desc">More choices = longer to decide. Reduce options at key decision points.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Fitts's Law</span><span class="desc">Target acquisition time depends on size and distance. Make interactive elements large enough.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Miller's Law</span><span class="desc">Working memory holds 7 plus or minus 2 items. Chunk information into groups of 5 to 9.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Jakob's Law</span><span class="desc">Users spend most time on other sites. Design to meet existing expectations.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Cognitive Load</span><span class="desc">Mental effort has a limit. Good design reduces extraneous load so users focus on the task.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Proximity</span><span class="desc">Elements close together are perceived as related. White space creates grouping without borders.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Similarity</span><span class="desc">Elements that look alike are perceived as functioning alike.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Closure</span><span class="desc">The brain completes incomplete shapes.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Enclosure</span><span class="desc">Bounded areas are perceived as groups. Cards work because of this.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Von Restorff</span><span class="desc">The different element is most remembered. Primary CTA buttons use a different colour for this reason.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Zeigarnik Effect</span><span class="desc">Incomplete tasks are remembered better. Progress bars create tension that motivates completion.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Peak-End Rule</span><span class="desc">Experiences judged by peak and end. The confirmation screen matters as much as the form.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Affordance</span><span class="desc">Appearance suggests function. Shadows signal a button is pressable.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Aesthetic-Usability</span><span class="desc">Attractive interfaces are perceived as easier to use, even when they are not.</span></div>
</div>

<div class="section" id="ui-fundamentals">
  <span class="kicker">Quick reference</span>
  <h2 class="section-heading">UI fundamentals</h2>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Visual hierarchy</span><span class="desc">Position, size, weight, colour. Important things go first. Without hierarchy, nothing stands out.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Typography</span><span class="desc">Size and weight create reading order. Bold and size differences create the sequence.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Colour</span><span class="desc">Draw focus or de-emphasise. Never colour alone. Support with text and icons for accessibility.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Grid</span><span class="desc">12-column grid standard for web. Elements align to the grid. White space separates columns.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">White space</span><span class="desc">Active design element. Reinforces grouping, reduces cognitive load.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Design patterns</span><span class="desc">Repeatable solutions. Reduce cognitive load through familiarity.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Consistency</span><span class="desc">Same patterns throughout. Users learn once and apply everywhere.</span></div>
  <div class="principle"><span class="name">Feedback</span><span class="desc">Every action gets a response — loading states, hover states, confirmations.</span></div>
</div>

</div>
</div>
<script>
const links = document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar nav a');
const sections = document.querySelectorAll('.section[id]');
const obs = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
  entries.forEach(e => {
    if (e.isIntersecting) {
      links.forEach(l => l.classList.remove('active'));
      const l = document.querySelector(`.sidebar nav a[href="#${e.target.id}"]`);
      if (l) l.classList.add('active');
    }
  });
}, { rootMargin: '-10% 0px -75% 0px' });
sections.forEach(s => obs.observe(s));
links.forEach(l => l.addEventListener('click', e => {
  e.preventDefault();
  document.getElementById(l.getAttribute('href').slice(1))?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' });
}));
</script>
</body>
</html>
